# NOTE: every credential below is a placeholder. Put your own values in .env, which is
# gitignored -- never here. A real password in a committed example is published the moment the
# repository is, and it stays in the history after it is edited out.
# Local development environment. Copy to .env, which is gitignored:
#
#   make env        (copies this file if .env does not exist yet)
#
# The Makefile loads .env and exports every name in it, so `make run` needs no typing
# and no shell-specific syntax. .env is read by make, not by the binary: the service
# still takes its configuration from the process environment only, so nothing here
# changes how it behaves in a deployment.
#
# Values are written without surrounding quotes. make passes the rest of the line
# verbatim, quotes included, so a quoted DSN arrives with the quotes still attached
# and fails as a connection error.

# The two runtime roles. Distinct on purpose: organization_app carries row-level
# security by app.tenant_id, organization_provider_app carries cross-Tenant scope.
# Using one for both would make every RLS test pass for the wrong reason.
ORGANIZATION_TENANT_DATABASE_URL=postgres://organization_app:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/organization_control_dev?sslmode=disable
ORGANIZATION_PROVIDER_DATABASE_URL=postgres://organization_provider_app:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/organization_control_dev?sslmode=disable

# Points at cmd/organization-devissuer (make issuer). Compared for exact equality,
# so a trailing slash here refuses every token and reads as a permissions bug.
ORGANIZATION_TOKEN_ISSUER=http://127.0.0.1:8098
ORGANIZATION_JWKS_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8098/certs
ORGANIZATION_TOKEN_AUDIENCE=organization-control

# Properties of the realm the deployment points at, which is why they are
# configuration and not constants.
ORGANIZATION_TENANT_CLAIM=https://scnehaux.com/tenant
ORGANIZATION_PROVIDER_ROLE=provider-admin

# A registered projection consumer may perform the authoritative fresh check without holding
# provider authority over every Tenant. Optional, and only together: unset, the two context routes
# stay provider-only. The dev issuer mints one with role=consumer.
ORGANIZATION_CONSUMER_ROLE=projection-consumer
ORGANIZATION_CONSUMER_CLAIM=https://scnehaux.com/consumer_id

ORGANIZATION_LISTEN_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1:8099

# Optional. The defaults are in internal/config/config.go; these are here so the
# knobs are discoverable rather than because local development needs them changed.
# ORGANIZATION_TOKEN_MAX_SKEW=60s
# ORGANIZATION_READINESS_TIMEOUT=2s
# ORGANIZATION_PROVISIONING_TIMEOUT=30m
# ORGANIZATION_PROVISIONING_RECONCILE_INTERVAL=15m
# ORGANIZATION_TENANT_NAME_MAX=120

# Integration tests connect as the owner rather than as either runtime role, because
# they create and drop schema, then reconnect as each runtime role to prove row-level
# security from the outside. Only the tests read these four.
#
# With TEST_DATABASE_URL unset the integration tests skip rather than fail, which is
# why REQUIRE_INTEGRATION exists: `make test-integration` sets it to 1 so a skip is a
# failure. A green suite that silently ran no integration test is the failure mode
# both variables exist to prevent.
TEST_DATABASE_URL=postgres://postgres:CHANGEME@localhost:5432/organization_control_dev?sslmode=disable
TEST_RUNTIME_PASSWORD=CHANGEME
TEST_PROVIDER_PASSWORD=CHANGEME
TEST_DISPATCH_PASSWORD=CHANGEME

# Keeps the Go build cache off a full C: drive.
GOTMPDIR=D:\Ansha\.gotmp
